  --- THE HEADLINE CONFIGURATIONS ---
  1. full-band weight + full-band noise        (the old baseline)
      RMSE    4.105 +- 0.142 deg   median   2.752 +-0.149 deg   improvement over config 1:    1.00x
  2. band-limited weight + full-band noise     (the reported fix)
      RMSE    0.307 +- 0.011 deg   median   0.200 +-0.012 deg   improvement over config 1:   13.39x
  3. band-limited weight + band-limited noise  (realistic, 20 Hz-8 kHz)
      RMSE    0.317 +- 0.011 deg   median   0.207 +-0.013 deg   improvement over config 1:   12.96x
  4. full-band weight + band-limited noise     (the defect's cost on realistic hardware, 20 Hz-8 kHz)
      RMSE    2.205 +- 0.087 deg   median   1.369 +-0.081 deg   improvement over config 1:    1.86x

  The improvement that survives on realistic hardware is config 4 -> 3, i.e. 6.96x,
  against 13.39x measured under the simulation's full-band noise model.

  Why band-limiting the weight still helps when the noise is already
  band-limited. Out-of-band weight budget, per acquisition bandwidth:
    acquisition band       bins outside 300-3400 Hz   f^2-weighted out-of-band
                              still at weight > 0.5   / in-band leverage ratio
    0-24 kHz (none)                          1522.8                     16.501
    20-20000 Hz                              1202.6                     12.328
    20-8000 Hz                                359.9                      3.102
    20-4000 Hz                                 74.7                      0.847
    300-3400 Hz                                31.8                      0.501
    Two mechanisms. (i) When the acquisition band is wider than the source
    band, the bins between 3400 Hz and the acquisition cut-off hold noise and
    no source but still receive unit weight. (ii) Even when the acquisition
    band equals the source band, gcc_phat zero-pads N -> 2N, and a brick wall
    on the length-N grid is not a brick wall on the length-2N grid: the odd
    bins carry sinc leakage. Those leaked bins sit at high frequency, where
    the f^2 leverage is large, which is why the residual penalty is not
    negligible. The 60 dB regularized-PHAT floor does not remove either.


================ C. SNAPSHOT SCALING, PAIRED ================
    N = 256 done
    N = 512 done
    N = 1024 done
    N = 2048 done
    N = 4096 done
  WHICH STATISTIC CARRIES THE SLOPE. At N = 256 the delay range spans 5 % of
  the record and both variants throw wrap outliers, so the RMSE at that point
  is set by a handful of catastrophic frames and its log-log slope is not a
  measure of the noise scaling. The theory (CRB, GDOP) is a statement about
  the DISPERSION of the bulk, so the slope is quoted on the robust standard
  deviation MAD/0.6745 of the signed error. Median, RMSE and the robust
  s.d. are all tabulated; medians are included because the archived log
  reports medians.
